<a name="listagg_to_string_agg"/><h1>listagg_to_string_agg</h1>

<p>The <b>listagg_to_string_agg</b> module rewrites Oracle/DB2-style <i>listagg()</i> aggregate calls as PostgreSQL <i>string_agg()</i> calls.  It's useful when running an application originally written for Oracle, DB2, or another database that supports <i>listagg()</i>, against PostgreSQL or another database that supports <i>string_agg()</i> but not <i>listagg()</i>.</p>

<p>A query like:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><font color="#008080">select</font> grp<font color="#990000">,</font> <b><font color="#000000">listagg</font></b><font color="#990000">(</font>name<font color="#990000">,</font><font color="#FF0000">','</font><font color="#990000">)</font> <font color="#008080">within</font> <b><font color="#000000">group</font></b> <font color="#990000">(</font>order by <font color="#008080">id</font> asc<font color="#990000">)</font>
from testtable group by grp</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>Would be translated to:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><font color="#008080">select</font> grp<font color="#990000">,</font> <b><font color="#000000">string_agg</font></b><font color="#990000">(</font><b><font color="#000000">cast</font></b><font color="#990000">(</font>name <font color="#008080">as</font> text<font color="#990000">),</font><font color="#FF0000">','</font> order by <font color="#008080">id</font> asc<font color="#990000">)</font>
from testtable group by grp</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>An example configuration:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	...
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> ... <b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		...
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;querytranslations&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;querytranslation</font></b> <font color="#009900">module</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"normalize"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;querytranslation</font></b> <font color="#009900">module</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"listagg_to_string_agg"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/querytranslations&gt;</font></b>
		...
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
	...
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>The module has no module-specific attributes - only the standard <b>module</b>, <b>enabled</b>, and <b>debug</b> attributes apply.</p>

<p>Note that, in this example, the normalize translation is also configured.  Loading normalize first helps the module identify <i>listagg()</i> calls more reliably, as their syntactic form (the spacing around <i>within group</i>, the parenthesized <i>order by</i> clause, etc.) is sensitive to whitespace and case.</p>

<p>Note that in this example, the normalize query translation is loaded prior to the listagg_to_string_agg query translation.  Pattern matching is substantially more reliable and efficient if the query has been normalized first.  See the <a href="normalize.html#normalize">normalize</a> query translation for more information.</p>

<h3>Considerations</h3>

<p>The module performs the following transformations on each <i>listagg(EXPR, SEP) within group (order by SORT)</i> call:</p>

<ul>
  <li>wraps the aggregated expression in <b>cast(... as text)</b>, because <i>string_agg()</i> requires a text-typed first argument while <i>listagg()</i> accepts any type that can be coerced to a string</li>
  <li>moves the <i>order by</i> from the <i>within group</i> clause inside the <i>string_agg()</i> argument list, which is where PostgreSQL expects it</li>
</ul>

<p>Calls inside string literals are left alone.  Identifiers like <i>mylistagg(...)</i> that merely end in <i>listagg</i> are also left alone.</p>

<p>The match is fairly strict.  A <i>listagg()</i> call is only rewritten if it is followed by '' within group ('' (a single space, then the keywords, then a space, then the open paren) and the contents of the <i>within group</i> clause begin with <i>order by </i> (lowercase, single space).  If you have queries with unusual spacing or case, run the <a href="normalize.html#normalize">normalize</a> translation first; otherwise the call will be passed through to the database untranslated and will fail there.</p>

<p>The module does not implement the <i>on overflow truncate</i> or <i>on overflow error</i> clauses, and does not implement <i>listagg()</i> used as a window function (//over (...)//).  Calls using either of these forms will not match and will be passed through untranslated.</p>

